# DOT DAY — MASTER PRODUCTION READINESS, FUNCTIONALITY AUDIT, BUG FIXING AND VERIFICATION PROMPT

## YOUR ROLE

Act as a senior Android engineer, software reliability engineer, QA automation engineer, and mobile application architect.

You are working on my EXISTING native Android application, **Dot Day**.

Your primary responsibility is to make the actual application operational, dependable, internally consistent, and thoroughly tested.

The app already contains its own architecture, UI, Room database, custom events, Jetpack Compose screens, Jetpack Glance home-screen widget, preferences, accent customization, date calculations, navigation, and widget appearance settings.

Treat the supplied project as the source of truth.

**Do not rebuild the app from scratch. Do not replace existing functionality with mockups. Do not convert it into a website. Do not remove features to make testing easier.**

I care more about a reliable application than an impressive-looking but incomplete application.

My goal is to have an app in which every existing button, setting, screen, action, preference, database operation, and widget interaction does what it is supposed to do.

One particularly important existing problem is that the **Refresh Widget** button does not work correctly for me. Investigate its entire execution path, establish the actual root cause, fix it, and verify the fix.

Do not assume the Refresh Widget button is the only defect. Audit the entire application systematically.

## NON-NEGOTIABLE PRIORITIES

Follow this priority order:

1. Preserve all existing user data and functionality.
2. Find and fix actual bugs and broken interactions.
3. Ensure settings, database state, UI state, and widget state remain synchronized.
4. Make every existing button and feature functional.
5. Verify date calculations, persistence, navigation, and widget behavior.
6. Make the application stable across relevant Android versions and widget launchers.
7. Preserve the established premium design and animations.
8. Add visual improvements only when they do not compromise correctness.

Do not introduce new features merely to make the project seem more complete. Finish the existing features first.

Do not declare the work complete simply because the application compiles or a preview looks correct.

